  "decisions": {
    "prefer_source": "When one photograph carries different classes in the two sources, the copy from --prefer-source survives. `clothes` is the default: the duplicate classes all sit in one Category (Sweter and Longsleeve are both Tops), so the choice cannot change which outfit the recommender builds, only the displayed name and the confusion matrix; Clothes_Dataset keeps its 15 classes at 500 and carries the vocabulary the UI shows; and it holds 2.3x the pixels of the CC0 clone.",
    "keep_thin_classes": "Longsleeve keeps its surviving photographs instead of being folded into Kaos. Topi ships with a comparable count today, so a thin class is a measurable weakness -- the per-class recall in the training report -- rather than a reason to delete a class the product shows.",
    "ambiguous_groups": "A group that disagrees with itself inside one source gets no survivor: every member is dropped and listed in conflicts.csv. Identical bytes filed under Jaket, Kemeja and Polo claim two of those labels are wrong, and picking one would invent an answer the data does not give.",
    "splits": "CC0 keeps the train/validation/test split it ships with; Clothes_Dataset has none, so each class folder is shuffled once with --seed and cut. Nothing is shuffled per photograph, so a re-run reproduces the same assignment and no photograph crosses a split.",
    "class_names": "The merged class list is the Indonesian vocabulary for all 18 classes, including the three Clothes_Dataset does not have: Kaos_Lengan_Panjang, Sepatu and Topi. Their rows in labels.json are tagged `gayaku/merged`, because the CC0 dataset itself only has the English spellings (Longsleeve, Shoes, Hat) that the bootstrap 10-class model uses. Every merged class therefore reaches the UI with the same name it would have had as a Clothes_Dataset folder, and gen_dart_labels.py needs no special case."
  },
